WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:06.300
Jason Alexander is unquestionably best known
for one role, but what a role it was: high-strung

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.780
lucky loser George Costanza on Seinfeld.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.820
He was just 29 when the show premiered in
1989.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.960
Playing an iconic character early in one's
career can be a double-edged sword.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.380
Sure, the job security, money, and being a
part of TV history are nice, but it's hard

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.650
for an audience to accept actors in other
roles after they're so closely associated

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:26.020
with one familiar character.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.460
Believe it or not, Alexander has had a colorful,
balanced, and sometimes glamorous career after

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:31.960
Seinfeld.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.560
Here's what he's been up to recently.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.780
Cursed to fail

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.100
For years, there was talk of a "Seinfeld Curse,"
which seemed to affect the former cast of

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.100
that classic comedy.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:48.380
Everyone's first post-Seinfeld projects tanked:
Julia Louis-Dreyfus' Watching Ellie, Michael

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.800
Richards' The Michael Richards Show, and a
pair of Jason Alexander shows.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.520
First up was Bob Patterson, a short-lived
2000 ABC sitcom about a motivational speaker

00:00:57.539 --> 00:00:59.320
whose personal life was a mess.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.940
"To my colleague, Bob Patterson, who is a
constant reminder that there is more to life

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:06.860
than success."

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:12.460
Then came Listen Up, which followed a fictionalized
version of sports radio host Tony Kornheiser,

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.780
who's a big shot at work but has a dysfunctional
personal life.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.100
Both shows lasted less than a year.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.200
Taking charge

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.020
Few people know episodic TV better than Jason
Alexander.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.740
Seinfeld featured more complicated direction
than most traditional sitcoms in the '90s.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.080
Thus, Alexander's experience on that show
makes him uniquely qualified to direct television,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.420
and that's what he does now.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.240
He's helmed episodes of everything from the
improv-heavy Campus Ladies to the single-camera

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.590
Everybody Hates Chris to tried-and-true sitcom
fare like Mike &amp; Molly, and even dramas like

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.440
Criminal Minds.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.200
He also directed and appeared in country star
Brad Paisley's music video for the hit song

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:51.920
"Online."

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.280
Song-and-dance Man

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.940
Before Seinfeld premiered, Alexander was a
theater guy.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:04.020
In the '80s, he starred in plenty of New York
stage fare, including Jerome Robbins' Broadway,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.580
which earned him a Tony Award for best actor
in a musical.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:13.100
After Seinfeld concluded in 1998, he returned
to the footlights, starring as Max Bialystock

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.120
in the first national tour of The Producers.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.140
He also notably had a starring role in the
2015 play Fish in the Dark, which was written

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.350
by Seinfeld co-creator Larry David.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.980
"If I came to see the play, would you want
me to come backstage?"

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.660
"Uh...you would not have to come backstage,
heres-"

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.500
"That's all I needed to hear!"

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.860
On the road, on stage

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.370
Like other well-known comic actors that came
before him, Alexander hit the stage with an

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.460
autobiographical one-person show, entitled
An Evening with Jason Alexander and His Hair.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.840
Described as a "hilarious re-telling of his
journey to and on the Broadway stage," Alexander

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.960
sings songs, tells jokes, and does a little
crowd work.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.980
He also wears a "completely bogus" hair piece
for the performance.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.540
He's toured the show off and on in casinos
and theaters around the world since 2014.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.400
On the road, on TV

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:11.120
There are so many networks cranking out high
quality TV these days that it's easy to overlook

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.220
even a show starring a Seinfeld veteran.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.520
Since the fall of 2017, Alexander has starred
on the Audience Network's Hit the Road, which

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.569
he co-created.

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:24.730
He plays a dad in search of higher meaning
in his life who forces his family into a rock

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.500
band-style tour bus to drive around the country.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:28.500
"Let's go!

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.540
Suit up and start zazzing, start zazzing,
suit it up!

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:32.040
Suit it up!"

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.420
The Israeli-Palestinian conflict

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.430
Alexander was raised in a Jewish home, and
he has worked extensively with a group aiming

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.580
to broker understanding in Israel.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:47.150
In 2004, he helped launch the "One Voice"
campaign, which enabled regular Palestinians

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:52.040
and Israelis living in disputed areas to vote
online for peace and settlement initiatives.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.480
Alexander's work with the campaign has included
public appearances and promotional videos.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:57.320
"Peace.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:58.320
Together.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:03:59.480
Now."

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.780
Too rich to work too hard

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.660
Alexander hit the showbiz jackpot with Seinfeld.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:10.620
Not only was it a creatively fulfilling role
on a hit sitcom for which he earned multiple

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.640
Emmy nominations and lasting fame, but he
was also handsomely paid.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:18.840
In the final season alone, he earned $600,000
an episode.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.810
Factoring in his cut from endless reruns on
syndication and online streaming, Alexander

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.080
is reportedly worth millions today.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.639
If we don't see him on screens too much anymore,
perhaps it's because he can afford to be selective

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.340
with his time.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.140
"Well, the jerk store called, they're running
out of you!"

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.080
"What's the difference, you're their all time
best seller!"

